\section*{Questions in Geometric Group Theory — Q 1.15}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a counterexample in the selected manuscript. A torsion-free hyperbolic group is claimed not residually finite and consequently not linear over any field.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} There is claimed to be a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group \(G\) whose finite residual is nontrivial:
\[
 R_f(G)=\bigcap_{\substack{Q\text{ finite}\\\phi:G\to Q}}\ker\phi\ne\{1\}.
\]
For this finitely generated example, the linear-obstruction corollary states
\[
 \forall K\text{ commutative field},\ \forall n\ge1,\ \forall\rho:G\to\operatorname{GL}_n(K),\qquad
 R_f(G)\subseteq\ker\rho.
\]
Thus a single nonidentity element is annihilated by every such finite-dimensional representation.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Punctured-line blocks with few short cycles give triangular relations meeting a geometric filling criterion. Real and prime-field rank comparisons produce a persistent finite-quotient obstruction, while local factorization certificates and planar metric bounds establish hyperbolicity and exclude torsion.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The torsion-free hyperbolic example has one fixed nonidentity element killed by every finite quotient, exactly refuting residual finiteness. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed negative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 252, the selected example is torsion-free hyperbolic and fails residual finiteness. Nonlinearity is a further group-theoretic consequence, not a separate selected conclusion. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{TorsionFreeHyperbolic.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Release075.main}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

